# DEFECT 2571: the lease handler killed a trap a library's own handler
# recovers (batch 38's lane b38-runtime, beside defect 2558, 2026-11-15).
#
# A library that installs its SIGTRAP and SIGILL handler from its
# constructor, before `main`, may make a trap on purpose and take it back by
# editing the interrupted context and returning, as a wasm runtime or a
# managed runtime embedded in C does. The runtime's lease handler, installed
# in `main`, found that handler, called it first, and then spoke and
# re-raised the signal whatever the handler had done: the program died at
# 133 with `the process is dying of SIGTRAP, under ...main, and this runtime
# did not raise it`, three runs of three on this Mac. Now the register file
# is read before the call and after it, and a handler that edited it has
# recovered: nothing is said and the thread goes on where it was sent.
#
# The library answers a trap by writing 5 into the result register (on
# x86-64 an `int3`, whose pc is already past it, so only the register
# moves), steps over an illegal instruction, does both again with its
# handler still in place, and then on a thread of its own
# (`fixedbugs-2571-library.h`).

extern "fixedbugs-2571-library.h"
    function lib_trap_answered() -> i64
    function lib_illegal_stepped() -> i64
    function lib_on_own_thread(task: i64) -> i64

function main()
    print("a trap answered: ", lib_trap_answered())
    print("an illegal instruction stepped over: ", lib_illegal_stepped())
    print("the trap again: ", lib_trap_answered())
    print("the illegal instruction again: ", lib_illegal_stepped())
    print("both on a thread of its own: ", lib_on_own_thread(task: 0) + lib_on_own_thread(task: 1))
